  "summary": "A style of Chinese painting originating in the Lingnan region, characterized by bright colors, Western techniques, and naturalistic subjects.",
  "description": "Features vibrant colors, bold brushwork, and a fusion of Chinese and Western artistic techniques, often depicting Southern Chinese flora and fauna. Developed as a reaction against the conservative literati tradition, embracing modernization while maintaining Chinese artistic sensibilities.",
  "culturalContext": "The Lingnan School — Gao Jianfu, Gao Qifeng and Chen Shuren (\"Two Gao One Chen\") — modernized Chinese painting from Guangdong (1900s-1940s): Japanese Nihonga-informed color-wash atmosphere, overseas subject matter (planes, volcanoes), and \"compromise between East and West\" ideology tied to Sun Yat-sen's revolution. The Second Generation (Guan Shanyue, Yang Shanshen, Zhao Shao'ang) built the Guangzhou academy lineage; Lingnan's mist-washed realism remains southern China's signature brush idiom.",
